Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(AliasTwin);
define
    avtC = 5;
    avtD;
    avtB  x=1;
    avtG  y=7;
    avtA;
    avtProbe code={
        avtD    = avtC;
        avtC    = 6;
        cerr "R0 scalar control            " avtD " (want 5 -- = is honest copy on a leaf)":;

        avtA    = avtB;
        avtB.x  = 2;
        t1      = avtA.x;
        cerr "R1 read A.x after B.x = 2    " t1 " (2 = ALIAS, 1 = TWIN)":;

        avtA.x  = 3;
        t2      = avtB.x;
        cerr "R2 read B.x after A.x = 3    " t2 " (3 = ALIAS, 2 = TWIN)":;

        cerr "R3 identity -- the two carriers":;
        showBody(avtA);
        showBody(avtB);
        cerr "R3 identity -- their x members":;
        showBody(avtA["x"]);
        showBody(avtB["x"]);

        avtA    = avtB;
        avtB    := avtG;
        t3      = avtA.y;
        cerr "R4 read A.y after B := G     " t3 " (7 = REBIND PROPAGATED, t3 = it did not)":;
        t4      = avtB.y;
        cerr "R4c control -- read B.y      " t4 " (MUST be 7, or R4 is VOID not negative)":;
        };
    ;
avtProbe();
cerr "ALIASTWIN SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

aliasTwinT -- does `A = B` on a group-carrying field ALIAS or TWIN.
Minted 2026-08-30. The last paper row before the no-unwrap recon.

WHAT EACH ROW IS FOR

R0 is the PROBE CERTIFICATION and it must run first. Every row below reads its
verdict through `=`, which is the operator under test, so without R0 the fixture
measures its subject with a copy of its subject. R0 mutates the source after the
copy: 5 means `=` is an honest copy on a scalar leaf, 6 would mean it aliased and
every row below it is uninterpretable rather than wrong.

R0 PRINTS avtD DIRECTLY, and that is deliberate. It is the one read in this file
that does NOT land in a named intermediate first, because a named intermediate
would be minted with the very operator R0 exists to certify -- the split would
put the subject inside the probe. Every row after R0 uses intermediates, which
is house style, and is legitimate precisely because R0 has banked the primitive.

R3 is the IDENTITY row and it is why the verdict is not simply "alias". R1 and R2
measure BEHAVIOUR, and behaviour cannot separate "one node, two holders" from "two
nodes over one storage". showBody is the only way to ask, per mintT's doctrine that
incant accessors are snapshot-by-value and two probes returning the same text prove
text equality and never node identity. It prints directly rather than through a
named intermediate because a pointer dump carries no verdict for an intermediate to
hold -- the house rule binds reads that FEED a verdict, and this one is the evidence
itself.

R1 and R2 are the question, in both directions. R2 is not decoration: a one-
directional result cannot tell a true alias from a share that only propagates
one way, and the two rows must agree on their verdict or neither is trustworthy.

⚠ WHY THE GROUP-SIDE READS STAY SINGLE-DOT AND ARE NOT SPLIT FURTHER.
THIS COMMENT IS LOAD-BEARING -- DO NOT HELPFULLY TIDY THE LINES IT DESCRIBES.
`avtB.x` is ONE hop, and that hop IS the probe: it is today's implicit
read-through doing the reaching, which is the regime being measured. Splitting
it into `hold = avtB;` first would inject a second `=` binding OF A GROUP
CARRIER -- a copy of the operation under test, sitting inside its own
measurement. So the file's own no-long-expressions rule stops at exactly one
hop here, for cause, and the cause is contamination rather than brevity.

THE ANSWER, read 2026-08-30, and it is NEITHER of the two names the row was
written to choose between:

    R0  5   -- instrument certified: it CAN see a twin when one exists
    R1  2   -- behaves as ALIAS
    R2  3   -- behaves as ALIAS in the mirror direction too, so = is not
               direction-asymmetric, which was its own candidate finding
    R3      -- carriers   avtA node/groupBody BOTH differ from avtB
               x members  DIFFERENT nodes, ONE SHARED groupBody
    R4  t3  -- `:=` through the original holder does NOT propagate
    R4c 7   -- and the rebind DID fire, so R4 is a negative and not a void

So `A = B` on a group carrier is a TWIN AT THE NODE LAYER AND AN ALIAS AT THE
BODY LAYER, and the body layer is the one every read and every write actually
touches. The member node is freshly minted; its groupBody -- where the data and
the list pointers live -- is not copied. That is the same condition mintT records
for the copy constructor ("it SHARES the body"), arriving here through = instead.

⚠ WHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN EITHER SIMPLE ANSWER WOULD HAVE. "Alias" would have
said change the binding; "twin" would have said nothing is owed. The measured
answer names a LAYER instead: the carriers are properly distinct, so A and B are
genuinely separate holders, and every surprise lives one level down in the shared
body. A ruling written against "alias" would have aimed at the wrong structure.

R4 IS THE EXTENSION ROW AND IT IS GENUINELY OPEN -- no outcome is pre-registered
here, deliberately. It asks what `:=` does to a carrier that `=` has already
handed to somebody else. R1-R3 established that `A = B` gives A a fresh node over
B's groupBody; R4 rebinds through the ORIGINAL holder and asks whether the new
holder sees it.

    reaches y (7)  the rebind wrote the SHARED BODY, so A re-pointed too
    reads "t3"     the rebind wrote B's OWN node, and A still holds the old group

⚠ READING THE SECOND OUTCOME. `t3` printing as the literal word "t3" IS the
nothing-answer, not a broken probe: opDot returns null, the local comes away with
no data, and a field with no data answers with its own tag. That is bear-trap #26
being useful for once, and it is why this row needs no separate null check.

⚠ R4c IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS WHY R4 IS ADMISSIBLE. A "did not propagate"
reading is indistinguishable from a rebind that never happened -- both leave A
without a y, and the nothing-answer looks identical. R4c reads y through the
holder that WAS rebound: 7 means the rebind fired and A genuinely did not follow;
anything else means R4 is VOID rather than negative, and says nothing at all.
Pair every nothing-expecting row with the sibling that must be non-empty.

EITHER ANSWER IS BANKED INTO THE SHARED-BODY DOCTRINE ROW as a stated consequence.
If it propagates, the row carries the warning label: A CARRIER RECEIVED BY `=` IS
A WINDOW, NOT A PHOTOGRAPH.

⚠ IT READ THE OTHER WAY, 2026-08-30, AND THE ROW TAKES NO WARNING LABEL. R4 = t3
with R4c = 7: the rebind fired through avtB and avtA did not follow. So the two
channels are CLEANLY SEPARATED, and that is the sentence the doctrine row carries
instead:

    `=`  shares the BODY      -- mutations through either holder are mutual
    `:=` re-points the HOLDER -- and reaches nobody else

A carrier received by `=` is a window onto the STORAGE and a photograph of the
BINDING. Which is the result that lets `:=` stand as the sole rebind channel
without a caveat: it cannot reach a holder it was never handed to.

